      "teams": [],
      "media": {
        "type": "image",
        "images": [
          {
            "url": "https://cdn.bsky.app/img/feed_fullsize/plain/did:plc:4xpj3xjxsxxcuh3ghaulzdqu/bafkreidh4bovlxsrhgimo6fr4zbjajffib5bzeae3lnhlvhxdv7s2cinmq",
            "alt": "Who will be the Most Improved Player of the Year?\n\nJones: Ausar Thompson. This could feel like a long shot considering there's a world where already good players (Brandon Miller, Dylan Harper) take another leap and get that level of recognition. With Thompson, it appears the Pistons have bet on him taking a stronger leap offensively by working to get the ball in his hands and moving him around the court. If he continues to showcase confidence to get to his spots and attack off the bounce, the math on the Pistons could change. Also the defense. All of the defense. \n\nIko: Brandon Miller. With LaMelo Ball and Miles Bridges gone, head coach Charles Lee can fully shift principles towards Kon Knueppel and Miller — with the latter as my bet to take home this award. The runway is there for an uptick in playmaking and overall offensive usage, combined with some sneaky defensive chops and rim protection as a 6-foot-9 combo-ish wing. There's some work to do regarding some of the types of shots he takes — he has a propensity to force things up when he's compromised — but Lee is one of the brightest young minds around and should assist in that.\n\nHaberstroh: Davion Mitchell. He slimmed down a ton in the offseason and looks like someone who's going to thrive next to Giannis Antetokounmpo in Erik Spoelstra's demanding uptempo system. Like NAW before him, Mitchell will shed his defensive specialist reputation and become a two-way force.\n\nDevine: Jaden McDaniels. Now, will he actually be notably and demonstrably better than he's been in recent seasons, where he's ticked up his scoring, shooting efficiency and complementary playmaking while consistently performing like an All-Defensive Teamer on the wing? Or will running alongside LaMelo Ball just get him the extra couple of buckets per game that make him a 20-plus-point scorer, which in turn makes everybody look a little closer at those year-over-year improvements? If it results in a trophy … who cares, I guess, right?"
          }
        ]
      },
